# Day 07 — lab/terraform/.gitignore
#
# Same hygiene as Days 05 and 06, with a Day 07 addition at the bottom.

# STATE. Plaintext JSON listing every resource this stack has touched.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfstate.backup
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# Provider cache. Hundreds of MB, machine-specific, regenerated by init.
.terraform/

# Saved plans can contain sensitive values in the clear.
*.tfplan
tfplan
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Real variable values. The .example file IS the documentation and IS committed.
terraform.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars
!terraform.tfvars.example

# Lambda zips built by the archive provider at plan time.
build/
*.zip

# ==========================================================================
# DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED: .terraform.lock.hcl
# ==========================================================================
# Commit it. It is what makes `terraform init` produce the same provider
# binary on your laptop, your colleague's laptop and in CI. Day 05's IAC-012
# is the check that catches a repo which ignores it.

# ==========================================================================
# DAY 07 ADDITION: FINDINGS, CREDENTIALS AND EVIDENCE
# ==========================================================================
#
# Three kinds of file this lab produces that must never reach a repository.
#
# GUARDDUTY AND SECURITY HUB FINDINGS contain the thing an attacker would most
# like to have: your account id, your instance ids, your VPC and subnet
# layout, your principal names, and a list of which of your controls are
# currently failing. A findings export is a reconnaissance report you wrote
# for them.
#
# ROTATION OUTPUT. `describe-secret` is safe; `get-secret-value` is not, and
# the difference is one word on a command line at 2am.
#
# CLOUDTRAIL OBJECTS pulled down for analysis are a complete record of your
# control plane, including every principal that has touched the account.
findings.json
findings-*.json
findings.csv
securityhub-*.json
guardduty-*.json
secret-*.json
*.credentials
cloudtrail-*/
AWSLogs/
